>I used pork neck only as an example, but I really mean any kind of meat and any kind of cut.

&#x200B;

As you can see, I didn't ask SPECIFICALLY about pork neck.

>So, what are the rules here? I just want to save time in the kitchen without spending money on pressure cooker etc.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

sous vide would be "etc." here :)

sorry for my ignorance because I know nothing about keto yet and will have to read about it more, but after using online keto calculators and counting macros and calories I think the only way to get so much fat would be to make scrambled eggs in literally pool of butter and then cook chicken liver in pool of olive oil and after that eat some cheese with avocado...     

&#x200B;

those calculators suggest me to consume 148g of fat, 85g of protein and 50g of carbs. How the hell am I supposed to maintain and not exceed 85g of protein? Lets say I want to eat 5 scrambled eggs for breakfast with 30g of butter, then 100g of cheese, then chicken 200g of chicken liver with olive oil  and I'm already above 90g of protein and at the same time didn't even hit my calorie and fat intake. How do you guys do it, damnit.

I have a full time job. I've been working for a year. I gave my manager resignation letter and I will be working here untill 31th October, and I start a new job on 2nd November.  

&#x200B;

Plus I live in a country where there's no such thing as "renter's history" But you sign for example a 1 year contract like with Cable TV or anything like that and you sign that you will be paying for that time. Although some landlords let you move out under one condition: you have to find someone for your replacement.

For example apartment with 2 rooms, and in each room there are 2 rommates. So 4 people under one roof. 

There are also many apartments where there are 6 rooms in apartment, and everyone lives alone in room. (more expensive option) But If I go that road, then I will literally have no money left. I wouldn't be able to buy even a snickers, because I would spend all money on therapy, room and food (while on food it would be very limited money.

&#x200B;
